abstract: >-
  Code, derived data and an interactive web app for a study of how the effect of
  rain on cycling in Zurich changed between 2011 and 2026. Open bicycle,
  pedestrian and car counts from the City and Canton of Zurich are joined with
  MeteoSwiss weather and analysed with Poisson regressions with fixed effects;
  the analysis plans and their predictions were fixed with checksums before the
  corresponding analyses. A wet day removed 33% of daytime cyclists in 2011–2014
  and 29% in 2022–2026, walking did not become less sensitive to rain, and car
  traffic is about 1.5% lower on wet days with no change since 2012. The
  repository also contains next-day forecasts of bike counts with a graph neural
  network (Graph WaveNet), LightGBM and a recurrent network. A paper based on
  this work is under review at a peer-reviewed journal. Version 1.1.0 adds a
  companion working paper on forecasting when the weather effect drifts:
  a model trained once on 2011–2013 under-predicts wet days by about ten
  percentage points a decade later, while models trained from 2014 onwards
  and models retrained every year do not.
